Statements:
[
    "Retriever failures can be grouped into three categories: coverage/completeness, relevancy, and ranking/order.",
    "Coverage/completeness is about how much of the total relevant context the retriever returns.",
    "The metric for coverage/completeness is contextual recall.",
    "Tunable parameters for coverage/completeness include chunking hyperparameters such as chunk size and overlap, TopK, the embedding model, and how chunks are made.",
    "If recall is low, increase chunk size, increase TopK, or change the embedding model to better encode content.",
    "Relevancy is about how relevant retrieved chunks are to the query.",
    "The metric for relevancy is contextual relevancy.",
    "Tunable parameters for relevancy include chunk size, chunking strategy, overlap, TopK, and the embedding model.",
    "If relevancy is low, decrease chunk size, try different embedding models, and make chunks more focused.",
    "Ranking/order is about the correctness of the ordering of retrieved results.",
    "The metric for ranking/order is precision, which is used to study ranking.",
    "Tunable parameters for ranking/order include retriever scoring settings such as TopK, and whether a reranker is added, including the reranker model and its settings.",
    "If ranking is poor, add or tune a reranker so it can re-score results and improve order, and adjust retriever/TopK settings.",
    "Chunking and the embedding stage are the main levers that affect retriever accuracy.",
    "Metrics are applied together across the application, but each targets a different component so components can be tweaked individually when one metric is low.",
    "Precision often targets the reranker.",
    "If no reranker is run in production, precision may be less relevant."
] 

